A||N||5||chris6878||Temp Guage and Gas Gauge not working.....?||||5||
Z||000000||chris6878||01-01-2000||04:07 AM||||Both gauges are giving me problems....   The temp gauge is turn upward... out of site.... cant even see the needle...   I grounded both wires on the sensor... (i was told this should cause the gauge to show hot) and nothing happened...   <P>My Gas Gauge isnt working either..  It looks as if it is stuck a little above 1/4 of a tank..   it moved once to way past full..  but thats all the movement i have seen from it..          any suggestions on fixing either problem?     <P>Thanks..<BR>chris||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||01-01-2000||10:44 AM||||I'm thinking that the temp. and gas gauge are basically one unit. If part of the gauge is rotated up were you can't see it, there's definitely a problem. You're going to have to pull the instrument cluster out to check it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||01-01-2000||10:53 AM||||My temp. gauge just did the same thing, but the gas gauge seems o/k.  I suspect our Temp. gauge problem is related to the factory wiring mistake, where the lamp test voltage appears across the gauge while the engine is cranking, as explained elsewhere in the Forum.  Since its warmer out today, I will have a look and see if its just the needle off its shaft. Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||01-01-2000||11:01 AM||||Sounds like the guage has stuck. What happens is the foam dust gasket around the guage over time turns into a sticky mess and will eventually capture the needle when it pegs out on start up.<BR>Only solution is to open it up and clean the old gasket off and make a new dust gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||01-03-2000||05:48 PM||||Wolfhound was right!  My temp. gauge needle was stuck in the dust gasket goo. I loosened the clear plastic gauge pod cover, and it popped back down.  So it couldnt happen again, I rewired, by crossing the gauge and the Hi temp. lamp both at the pod and the sensor/switch. (See Haynes, page 286, bottom) The sensor/switch was easy, because its in the cylinder head, beside the coil, and just above the exh. manifold. Like others, I found it cooked good, so it was easy to cut away the remaining plastic of the plug, and swap the connections. (mark at least one of the wires) The pod is very tricky, and involves cutting and splicing wiring at both printed circuit board plugs.  I have sketches of what I did, and will post again when I figure out how to scan them etc.  Not something you should attempt if you are not comfortable with wiring schematics and soldering irons!!!  Still, it was worth the effort, knowing my gauge will never stick again. Its a treat to see the high temp. lamp come on while cranking, and the gauge stay where it should be at the low end of the scale. Dave||||reg||1
Z||000005||chris6878||01-04-2000||01:56 AM||||thanks everyone       Wolfhound and daved...  u were absolutely correct about the temp guage...   i took the clear cover off enough to hit the needle with a screwdriver...   works perfect now...  it was just stuck as u guys said...    thanks alot for the info...<P><BR>now if i could just figure out my gas gauge..<P>get this...  I filled her up today..  the gauge is constantly at 1/4 of a tank...   after pumping..  i turn the ingnition on.. and the gauge moves alway  to a little over the full gauge...     but then   went right back to the 1/4  tank mark in a few seconds... hmmmmmm???<P>||||reg||7
